# FRONTIER 5.1 — copy this file to `.env` and fill in the keys you have.
# `.env` is where your keys live. Never share it, never commit it, never paste it into a chat.
#
# You do NOT need every service. Any feature whose key is missing is shown in red in
# Options, and everything else still works. The setup we recommend, cheapest first:
#   Gemini free tier ..... watching YouTube footage for free (about a video a day)
#   OpenLux .............. the cheapest backup when the free tier runs out
#   Algrow ............... pictures, voices, subtitles — and niche research
#   WaveSpeed + kie.ai ... the AI presenter, photo cutouts, real objects
# Keys from any other provider work too: send Claude Code the link to its API page.

# ── GOOGLE GEMINI — watches the YouTube footage. FREE up to 8 hours of video a day. ──
# aistudio.google.com/apikey -> Create API key. Google never charges a free-tier key:
# when the day's quota is used, it refuses and Frontier moves on to OpenLux.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ── OPENLUX — the same Gemini models at a fraction of Google's price: the backup watcher ──
# openlux.ai. Frontier tries watchers gemini -> openlux -> algrow, so with these two keys
# Algrow is never asked to watch a video (see README -> Cost).
OPENLUX_API_KEY=
OPENLUX_BASE_URL=https://api.openlux.ai
# OPENLUX_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite

# ── WHICH SERVICES MAY WATCH FOOTAGE, AND HOW MUCH ──
# YT_WATCHERS: the only video models allowed to watch YouTube footage, comma-separated
# (gemini, openlux, algrow, wavespeed, kie). Empty = every service with a key, in that order.
# "gemini" = the free tier only: when its quota runs out, videos are skipped instead of paid for
# elsewhere — the video then uses fewer YouTube shots and more stock footage.
YT_WATCHERS=
# YT_MAX_CANDIDATES: at most this many videos watched per search, whatever the channel asks for
# (the channels ask for 3-4). 2 watches roughly a third to a half less footage. Empty = the channel's own.
YT_MAX_CANDIDATES=

# ── ALGROW — pictures (gpt-image-2), voice (ElevenLabs), subtitles, niche research ──
# go.algrow.online/tool  (affiliate link — it costs you nothing extra and supports Frontier)
# Buy credits as top-ups: a picture then costs about a cent.
ALGROW_API_KEY=

# ── WAVESPEED — the AI presenter (hosting, InfiniteTalk), photo spotlight, real objects,
#    depth pop, Vox scenes; also backup voice and pictures ──
# wavespeed.ai -> Dashboard -> Access Keys. Paid per run from a prepaid balance.
WAVESPEED_API_KEY=

# ── KIE.AI — renders the default AI presenter (Kling Avatar 2.0); backup pictures, a
#    background remover and a Claude relay. The AI presenter needs this AND WaveSpeed. ──
# kie.ai -> API Key
KIE_API_KEY=

# ── AI33 — voice, if you already use it ──
AI33_API_KEY=
AI33_VOICE_ID=

# ── EPIDEMIC SOUND (optional) — each video's own music and effects, from YOUR Epidemic Sound account ──
# Your account's API key (it starts with epidemic_live_). With it, every chapter gets a track chosen by its
# mood and cut to the chapter's exact length, and each channel a fresh effects kit. What it downloads is
# licensed to your account for your own videos. Without it: the tracks you put in assets/music/ and
# Frontier's synthesised effects.
EPIDEMIC_API_KEY=

# ── PEXELS — stock footage (free, instant, no card): https://www.pexels.com/api/ ──
PEXELS_API_KEY=
# ── PIXABAY — more free stock video + photos, used next to Pexels: https://pixabay.com/api/docs/ ──
PIXABAY_API_KEY=

# ── SCRIPTS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "claudecode" writes scripts through the Claude Code you already have — no extra key.
# "anthropic" calls the API directly and needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.
CLAUDE_PROVIDER=claudecode
SCRIPT_PROVIDER=claudecode
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ── WHICH SERVICE DOES WHAT (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Empty = the first service with a key: Algrow, then WaveSpeed, then ai33 / kie.
# A style file can also name its own voice provider.
VOICE_PROVIDER=
IMAGE_PROVIDER=
# ElevenLabs model on Algrow. Keep v3: it performs the [pause] / [whispering] tags scripts use.
ALGROW_TTS_MODEL=eleven_v3
ALGROW_IMAGE_MODEL=gpt-image-2
# One voice for every channel (normally each style file has its own):
ALGROW_VOICE_ID=
# The voice name used when WaveSpeed speaks (ElevenLabs v3 voices: Brian, Alice, ...):
# WAVESPEED_VOICE=Brian
# WAVESPEED_IMAGE_MODEL=bytedance/seedream-v4

# ── YOUTUBE FOOTAGE (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Path to a yt-dlp you installed yourself, if it isn't found automatically:
# YTDLP_BIN=
# Gemini model for footage logging with GEMINI_API_KEY (default: the newest Flash that answers):
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.8-flash
#
# "Sign in to confirm you're not a bot"? A 40-minute video asks YouTube for hundreds of things in an hour
# and YouTube answers a burst like that by blocking. These make the asking polite — start with the first two:
# YT_SLEEP=2                   seconds between requests
# YT_WORKERS=2                 yt-dlp runs at once (normally 6 to 8)
# YTDLP_COOKIES_FROM_BROWSER=chrome    sign in as yourself (chrome, brave, edge, firefox, safari)
# YTDLP_COOKIES=                       ...or the path to an exported cookies.txt instead
# YTDLP_PLAYER_CLIENT=web_safari       last resort: ask as another client. It costs resolution —
#                                      web_safari tops out at 720p, mweb and tv_simply at 360p, and
#                                      anything under 480 lines is thrown away as too small.
# YTDLP_PROXY=                         your own proxy, if you have one
# YTDLP_ARGS=--force-ipv4              anything else passed straight to yt-dlp
# Use a spare Google account for the cookies, never the one your channel lives on. Fewer candidates per
# window (look.youtube.candidates in the style file) and "sources": ["youtube","archive"] — the Internet
# Archive asks nothing of you — cut the number of requests at the source.

# ── THE RENDER FARM (optional) — render on rented machines instead of this one ──
# One machine per video; each renders, uploads and switches itself off. See
# README-RENDER-FARM.md. `python -m farm.farm check` tests all of this and rents nothing.
#
# WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU START: at home, scripts and edit decisions go through the
# Claude Code you are already signed into, free. A rented machine has no such login, so
# those calls become paid Anthropic API calls. Frontier writes the SCRIPT on your own
# computer for exactly this reason, but the rest still costs — watch your first video.
# runpod.io -> Settings -> API Keys
RUNPOD_API_KEY=
# Cloudflare R2 (recommended: nothing to pay to download your own videos back).
# Any S3 service works — Backblaze B2, Wasabi, AWS — put its endpoint here instead.
FARM_BUCKET_ENDPOINT=
FARM_BUCKET=
FARM_BUCKET_KEY_ID=
FARM_BUCKET_SECRET=
# FARM_BUCKET_REGION=auto
#
# The machine. Frontier renders on PROCESSORS, never the graphics card (Chromium draws
# with --disable-gpu, ffmpeg encodes with libx264) — a cheap GPU pod is simply RunPod's
# least expensive way to rent eight cores. `python -m farm.farm machines` lists them.
# FARM_GPU=NVIDIA RTX A4000
# FARM_VCPU=8
# FARM_MIN_RAM=24
# FARM_CLOUD=COMMUNITY            COMMUNITY is cheaper; SECURE is more reliably available
# FARM_DISK_GB=150                working files are far bigger than the finished video
# FARM_CPU=                       a CPU-only pod instead (cpu3g / cpu5c); caps at 8 cores
#
# The safety rails. A pod that finishes and stays on is the only way this gets expensive.
# FARM_MAX_PODS=5                 how many machines at once
# FARM_MAX_MINUTES=240            a watchdog inside each pod kills it at this
# FARM_BUDGET_USD=10              a batch that costs more than this is stopped
#
# Prebuilt image, so a pod starts in a minute instead of five (see the Dockerfile):
# FARM_IMAGE=you/frontier-render:1

# ── OPTIONAL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FRONTIER_PORT=7860
# VIDEO_W=1920
# VIDEO_H=1080
# FPS=30
# Frontier watermarks nothing. For your own corner QR, drop it at assets/qr.png and set 1.
# QR_ENABLED=0
